Chapter 3 – The Civilizational Design Grammar: Formal Structure and Computational Core

The Civilizational Design Grammar is a shape grammar extended with symbolic metadata, environmental coupling functions, and manufacturing constraints. It inherits the mature literature on Islamic geometric pattern generation—specifically the Kaplan–Salesin method for star patterns—and fuses it with the parametric logic of muqarnas as a three‑dimensional transition geometry. The result is a computationally tractable system that generates habitat configurations from a small set of culturally meaningful production rules.

3.1 Shape Grammar with Semantic Metadata

A production rule in the CDG is a 5‑tuple:

R = \langle L, \rightarrow, R, \Sigma, \Phi, \mathbf{c} \rangle

where:

· L is the left‑hand shape (a spatial region or motif primitive);
· R is the right‑hand replacement shape (an affine‑transformed assembly of elements);
· \Sigma encodes spatial and manufacturing constraints (overhang angles, minimum feature size, structural continuity);
· \Phi is a semantic tag drawn from an Amazigh ontology (protection, fertility, hospitality, cosmic order, transition);
· \mathbf{c} is a context vector that encodes the adjacent motif types, spatial location, and hierarchical position. This vector resolves polysemy: a diamond may mean “protection” on a radiation shield but “clan identity” on an interior partition.

A simple example illustrates the generative mechanism. The protective muqarnas‑diagrid rule applies to the cylindrical‑core‑to‑dome junction region \Omega_j:

\begin{aligned}
L &: \Omega_j \\
R &: \text{muqarnas cell cascade } \{C_i\} \text{ with tier count } n, \text{ cell depth } d_c = f(\Phi, D(\mathbf{x})) \\
\Sigma &: \text{overhang angle } \le 45^\circ \text{ for unsupported printing; structural continuity} \\
\Phi &: \{\text{protection}, \text{cosmic boundary}, \text{transition}\}
\end{aligned}

The cell depth d_c is not a constant; it is a function of the local ionising radiation dose field D(\mathbf{x}) computed from a lunar radiation environment model:

d_c(\mathbf{x}) = d_{\min} + (d_{\max} - d_{\min}) \cdot e^{-k \cdot D(\mathbf{x})}

Where the radiation is most intense, the muqarnas cells grow deeper, increasing the effective shielding mass. The cultural pattern’s morphological intensity thus directly tracks the engineering demand. The motif does not merely symbolise protection; it performs it.

3.2 The Lunar Zellige Field

Two‑dimensional ornament is generated through a continuous implicit function that encodes the star‑pattern logic of Moroccan zellige. Using the Kaplan–Salesin methodology, a fundamental region of a symmetry group—p4m for square grids, p6m for hexagonal—is populated with a set of line segments that are reflected and rotated by the group’s isometries to tile the plane. The CDG extends this into a continuous field:

P(\mathbf{x}) = \sum_{i=1}^{N} A_i \sin(\mathbf{k}_i \cdot \mathbf{x} + \phi_i) \, \Gamma_i(\mathbf{x})

Here, \mathbf{k}_i are wave vectors drawn from the reciprocal lattice of the chosen symmetry group; A_i encodes the local amplitude, which translates directly into the thickness of deposited regolith‑based material; and \Gamma_i(\mathbf{x}) is an envelope function that confines each sinusoidal component to a structural rib zone. A positive amplitude produces a raised ridge; a negative amplitude a groove. The entire field is subject to manufacturing constraints: all resulting features must have width \ge 10 cm (the robotic deposition limit) and overhang angle \le 45^\circ.

This formulation unites three traditionally separate concerns: the visual identity of the Amazigh diamond‑and‑star vocabulary, the structural reinforcement of the pressure shell through ribbing, and the enhancement of radiation scattering through micro‑geometry. It is, in a precise sense, a structural‑cultural composite.

3.3 Hybrid Girih for Dense Lattice Generation

In addition to pure star patterns, the CDG incorporates a hybrid girih generation method. A girih is the underlying line network of an Islamic geometric pattern; by superposing the girih sets from two distinct symmetry groups—say, p4m and p6m—a denser, self‑similar lattice emerges. This lattice is particularly well‑suited to situations where the habitat shell must carry high compressive loads while maximising radiation scattering path length. The hybrid girih algorithm runs as follows:

1. Generate two independent star patterns via the Kaplan method, each with its own rosette parameter.
2. Extract the underlying girih line families from each pattern.
3. Compute the union of the two line sets.
4. Apply manufacturing constraints to the combined network.

The output is a structurally optimised, culturally resonant rib network that can be fed directly to the path‑planning module of a robotic regolith printer.

3.4 Muqarnas Diagrid Transition Grammar

The transition from a cylindrical habitat core to a hemispherical regolith‑shielded roof poses a classical architectural problem: stepping a load and a visual field from a polygonal or circular plan to a dome. This is precisely the problem that muqarnas vaulting solved in the monumental architecture of the Islamic world. The CDG formalises a Muqarnas Diagrid Transition Grammar that generates a load‑bearing cascade of inclined cells.

Each cell is a four‑sided pyramidal frustum. Tiers are stacked with a fixed angular rotation, creating a spiral‑like cascade. The geometry is governed by the parameters:

· N_t: number of tiers (typically 5–8);
· R_{\text{base}}: cylinder core radius;
· R_{\text{top}}: dome spring line radius;
· \theta: cell wall inclination from vertical (30°–45°);
· h_c = (R_{\text{top}} - R_{\text{base}}) / N_t;
· \Delta r = h_c \tan \theta.

The radiation‑shielding advantage of this geometry is substantial. For a normally incident galactic cosmic ray particle, the slant path length through an inclined wall of normal thickness t is:

l_{\text{slant}} = \frac{t}{\cos\theta}

At \theta = 45^\circ, a 1‑metre thick wall yields l_{\text{slant}} \approx 1.4 m, increasing the effective areal density by 40% without additional mass. When a ray passes through multiple cells at different angles, the total column density is the sum of contributions:

\rho_{\text{eff}}(\mathbf{r}, \Omega) = \sum_j \rho_{\text{mat}} \cdot \frac{t_j}{\cos\theta_j(\Omega)}

This is evaluated by ray‑tracing in the three‑dimensional model, and the CDG optimiser adjusts t and \theta to ensure that for all interior points, the minimum areal density exceeds the established benchmark of 100 g/cm² (approximately 2 metres of regolith equivalent). The muqarnas diagrid therefore serves four inseparable functions: it carries dome loads to the cylindrical walls, it scatters radiation, it provides a visually complex cultural ceiling surface, and it offers attachment points for interior lighting and the symbolic wayfinding layer.

3.5 Tifinagh Parametric Wayfinding

Spatial orientation in a confined, repetitive habitat is a known human‑factors challenge. The CDG encodes the Amazigh Tifinagh script as a parametric type system for wayfinding and spatial identity. Each character is generated from the same geometric engine as the zellige and muqarnas, so that the stroke weight, curvature, and colour track the local manufacturing constraints. A glyph for “habitation” (ⴰ) may be engraved into the regolith plaster of a crew quarter wall; the glyph for “airlock/transition” (ⵣ yaz) is embossed on the inner hatch of every pressure door. Because Tifinagh is inherently geometric—composed of straight lines, circles, and diamonds—it prints cleanly at the 10‑centimetre scale and can be read both visually and tactilely, serving as a backup orientation cue in the event of lighting failure.

The symbolic wayfinding layer extends beyond individual glyphs. Directional sequences of Tifinagh characters form continuous friezes along corridor walls, guiding crew from private quarters to work areas, from the galley to the prayer space, from the agadir storage cluster to the bioregenerative courtyard. The result is a habitat that is readable as a cultural landscape, where movement through space is simultaneously movement through a lattice of shared meaning.

