Chapter 6 – Environmental Systems: The Lunar Courtyard as a Closed Ecological-Emotional Loop

The lunar riad courtyard is the most complex node in the CDG, because it is where the grammar’s geometric, environmental, and semantic layers converge most intensely. This chapter provides the detailed environmental coupling model and demonstrates that the courtyard can function as a stable, resilient core of the habitat’s life‑support architecture.

6.1 Radiative‑Conductive Thermal Model

The courtyard’s thermal behaviour is governed by the energy balance:

\dot{Q}_{\text{internal}} + \dot{Q}_{\text{solar}} - \dot{Q}_{\text{radiator}} - \dot{Q}_{\text{wall conduction}} = 0

where \dot{Q}_{\text{internal}} is the heat load from crew metabolism, equipment, and lighting; \dot{Q}_{\text{solar}} is the solar gain admitted through the fibre‑optic daylighting system; \dot{Q}_{\text{radiator}} is the heat rejected to space; and \dot{Q}_{\text{wall conduction}} is the parasitic loss through the regolith shield. The radiator is a vacuum‑exposed plate of area A_{\text{rad}} with high‑emissivity coating, rejecting heat according to Q_{\text{out}} = \epsilon \sigma A_{\text{rad}} (T_{\text{wall}}^4 - T_{\text{space}}^4). The wall conduction term is minimised by the 2‑metre regolith overburden, whose extremely low thermal conductivity (~0.3–0.8 W/m·K) effectively decouples the interior from the surface temperature swings.

A transient simulation over a full lunar day (708 hours) shows that the atrium air temperature can be maintained between 22°C and 26°C with a radiator area of approximately 12 m² and a modest PCM buffer, without any mechanical refrigeration. The courtyard thus achieves the passive thermal stability that vernacular earth architecture provides on Earth, but through radiation rather than convection.

6.2 The Water Spiral Fountain as a Heat Exchanger and Cultural Anchor

The central fountain is not a frivolous water feature; it is the condenser stage of the habitat’s thermal control loop. Warm coolant from the habitat’s liquid‑cooled garment and avionics loops enters the base of the spiral and flows upward in a thin film over a regolith‑composite channel. The large surface‑area‑to‑volume ratio maximises convective heat transfer to the atrium air. From the top of the spiral, the cooled liquid is recirculated. A small fraction is diverted to the plant walls as irrigation, carrying dissolved nutrients from the greywater processing system.

Psychoacoustic research has established that the sound of gently moving water at frequencies between 500 Hz and 4 kHz reduces cortisol levels and improves cognitive performance in confined environments. The fountain’s acoustic signature is tuned by adjusting the channel geometry—the same parametric engine that generates the zellige pattern controls the rib spacing that breaks the water film into a desired sound spectrum. The fountain thus serves as a non‑pharmaceutical stress countermeasure, operating continuously as the habitat’s “heartbeat.”

6.3 Daylighting and the Circadian Simulation

The fibre‑optic daylighting system that feeds the courtyard solves two problems simultaneously. First, it delivers full‑spectrum solar light to the deepest parts of the habitat without the mass penalty of large windows or the thermal penalty of direct solar ingress. Second, it enables a programmable circadian cycle that anchors crew physiology to a 24‑hour Earth day, despite the Moon’s 708‑hour diurnal cycle.

The heliostat array on the surface tracks the Sun during the lunar day. The collected light is filtered through a pair of rotating polarisers that control intensity and spectral content. During “day” hours, the light is warm (correlated colour temperature ~4000 K). As “evening” approaches, the spectrum shifts to a cooler blue‑white (~6500 K) to suppress melatonin, then fades to a deep amber before “sleep.” During the lunar night, the system switches to a culturally meaningful LED array that projects Amazigh constellation patterns onto the courtyard ceiling—patterns that encode seasonal information and mythological narratives. The night‑time palette uses cobalt blue (the Amazigh colour of refuge), saffron yellow (sunlight memory), and soft white (purity), maintaining a connection to the chromatic cosmology of the Atlas.

6.4 Bioregenerative Integration

The courtyard’s plant walls are not merely decorative. They are integrated into the environmental control and life‑support system (ECLSS) as a supplementary bioregenerative unit. The selected species—mint, coriander, rosemary, dwarf date palm—are chosen for a combination of reasons: they are edible and medicinally relevant in North African tradition; they have moderate transpiration rates that can be managed by the ECLSS; and they carry strong cultural associations with hospitality (mint tea), fertility (date palm), and purification (rosemary).

The evapotranspiration load is modelled by:

\dot{m}_{\text{ET}} = \sum_i A_{\text{leaf},i} \cdot g_{s,i} \cdot (e_{\text{sat}}(T) - e_{\text{air}})

where A_{\text{leaf}} is the leaf area, g_s is the stomatal conductance, and the vapour pressure deficit drives the flux. This moisture is recovered by the dehumidifier and fed back into the water loop. The carbon dioxide fixed by the plants is approximately 0.3 kg/day for 15 m² of growing area—roughly one‑third of one crew member’s respiratory output. This does not close the life‑support loop, but it provides a tangible, visible, edible supplement that transforms the ECLSS from an invisible machine into a living, breathing part of the home.

