Chapter 8 – Ethical Governance and Co‑Authorship

A grammar that generates culturally derived designs without the ongoing consent and participation of the originating community is not a tool of preservation; it is a new form of colonial extraction. This chapter describes the ethical infrastructure that makes the CDG a living cultural medium rather than a static, appropriative catalogue.

8.1 The Amazigh Lunar Design Council

The ALDC is the sovereign advisory body for all CDG applications. Its composition is mandated by the grammar’s own governance protocol:

· Three master artisans (zellige, textile, woodwork);
· Two cultural linguists or Tifinagh specialists;
· Two elders or knowledge keepers;
· Two community representatives (youth, diaspora);
· One space architect (facilitator, non‑voting).

The Council operates under principles aligned with the CARE framework for Indigenous data governance: Collective Benefit, Authority to Control, Responsibility, and Ethics. It holds veto power over the inclusion of any symbol in the CDG library and over its spatial assignment within a habitat. No engineering trade‑off can override a Council veto.

8.2 Motif Library Governance

Each motif in the CDG’s digital asset library carries a metadata record that includes:

· A unique identifier and provenance information (region, community, artisan lineage, date of documentation);
· Access level: Open, Restricted (requiring per‑instance Council approval), or Sacred (only permitted in designated prayer or meditation spaces);
· A list of approved semantic tags \Phi with context vectors;
· A complete change history, logging every re‑semanticisation proposal and its disposition.

The library is version‑controlled on a secure server with access tiers. The CDG software itself is released under an open‑source licence, but the motif data layer is access‑controlled by the Council, effectively decoupling the grammar’s generative engine from the cultural data it consumes.

8.3 The Re‑Semanticisation Meta‑Rule

Meaning is not frozen. A symbol that once meant “protection from the evil eye” might, after years of lunar habitation, come to mean “protection from solar particle events.” The CDG explicitly accommodates this evolution through a re‑semanticisation meta‑rule:

After any mission of sufficient duration to produce statistically meaningful CRI data, the crew and the ALDC shall convene a deliberative assembly to review the lived semantic experience of the habitat. Any crew member may propose an updated \Phi tag for a motif, with supporting testimony. The Council deliberates and, by a two‑thirds majority, may ratify the change. The provenance ledger records the update, preserving the original meaning as a historical layer.

This meta‑rule transforms the CDG from a preservation archive into a dialogic medium, where the first generation of lunar settlers becomes co‑author of the cultural language rather than its passive recipient.

8.4 Benefit Sharing and Intellectual Property

While traditional Amazigh knowledge is held collectively and inalienably, any commercial application of CDG‑derived designs—for example, Earth‑based building products that license a zellige pattern generated by the grammar—must return a percentage of royalties to a community fund managed by the Council. The fund supports cultural documentation, educational programmes in Amazigh communities, and the training of the next generation of artisans. This provision ensures that the lunar application of heritage knowledge generates tangible, equitable benefit on Earth, closing the loop of reciprocity.

