Appendix B – Muqarnas Diagrid Transition Grammar: Technical Specifications

B.1 Geometry Description and Parameters

The muqarnas diagrid is a stacked‑tier cellular structure that transitions from a cylindrical pressure shell (radius R_{\text{base}}) to a hemispherical dome (spring‑line radius R_{\text{top}}). Each tier consists of identical cells arranged with m‑fold rotational symmetry.

Input parameters:

· N_t : number of tiers (5–8)
· R_{\text{base}} : cylinder core radius, e.g. 3.0 m
· R_{\text{top}} : dome spring‑line radius, e.g. 4.5 m
· \theta : cell wall inclination angle from vertical (30°–45°)
· m : rotational symmetry order (8 for octagonal, 12 for dodecagonal)
· t : nominal wall thickness normal to the cell face (0.25–0.40 m)

Derived dimensions:

· Tier height: h_t = (R_{\text{top}} - R_{\text{base}}) / N_t
· Radial offset per tier: \Delta r = h_t \tan\theta
· Cell base width (chord at inner radius): w_{\text{in}} = 2 R_{\text{base}} \sin(\pi/m)
· Cell top width (chord at outer radius): w_{\text{out}} = 2 (R_{\text{base}} + N_t \Delta r) \sin(\pi/m)

Each cell is a four‑sided pyramidal frustum; adjacent cells share faces. The assembly is statically determinate when the dome load is transferred through the cells to the cylindrical wall, provided that the cell‑to‑cell contact faces are in compression under internal pressurisation (50 kPa over vacuum). A finite‑element analysis (FEA) with regolith‑sintered material properties (compressive strength 20 MPa, density 2500 kg/m³) confirms structural adequacy for lunar gravity (1.62 m/s²) with a safety factor > 2.5 for t \ge 0.30 m.

B.2 Radiation Shielding Model

The effective slant path length for a normally incident GCR particle through a cell wall is:

l_{\text{slant}}(\theta) = \frac{t}{\cos\theta}

For a multi‑tier configuration, a ray from an interior point \mathbf{r} in direction \Omega may intersect multiple cell walls. The total areal density along that ray is:

\rho_{\text{eff}}(\mathbf{r}, \Omega) = \sum_{j=1}^{M} \rho_{\text{mat}} \cdot \frac{t_j}{\cos\theta_j(\Omega)}

where the sum runs over all intersected walls, \theta_j(\Omega) is the angle between the ray and the wall normal, and \rho_{\text{mat}} \approx 2500 kg/m³. A ray‑tracing module integrated into the CDG evaluates this quantity for a grid of interior points and a set of directions sampling the full sphere. The design constraint is:

\min_{\mathbf{r}, \Omega} \rho_{\text{eff}}(\mathbf{r}, \Omega) \ge 100\ \text{g/cm}^2 \quad (\text{equivalent to } \approx 2\ \text{m of regolith})

In practice, with t = 0.35 m and \theta = 40^\circ, a single‑wall slant path gives \rho_{\text{eff}} \approx 0.35 \times 2500 / \cos 40^\circ \approx 1140 kg/m² = 114 g/cm², meeting the requirement even before accounting for multiple wall intersections. The diagrid thus enhances shielding without requiring the full 2 m thickness everywhere, reducing printed mass.

B.3 Tier and Cell‑Depth Modulation by Radiation Field

The cell depth d_c (the distance from the interior face of a cell to its exterior apex) is modulated by the local radiation dose field D(\mathbf{x}) (Gy/yr) obtained from a lunar radiation environment model:

d_c(\mathbf{x}) = d_{\min} + (d_{\max} - d_{\min}) \cdot e^{-k \cdot D(\mathbf{x})}

Typical values: d_{\min} = 0.20 m, d_{\max} = 0.50 m, k = 0.01 (Gy/yr)⁻¹. The dose field D(\mathbf{x}) is highest at the polar regions (where the habitat may be located) due to lower horizon shielding, and is computed by a Geant4 simulation or by the CRÈME model. The modulation ensures that where the radiation is most intense, the cultural pattern thickens to provide greater protection—a direct semantic‑to‑physical mapping of the protection motif (\Phi = \text{protection}).

B.4 Interior Attachment System

The inward‑facing surfaces of the muqarnas cells incorporate a regular grid of M5 threaded inserts (stainless steel, cast into the regolith‑composite during printing) on a 0.20 m pitch. These inserts serve as mounting points for:

· LED lighting strips following the cell edges,
· Projection surfaces for the symbolic wayfinding overlay,
· Acoustic absorption panels (regolith‑fibre felt) clipped into the cell recesses,
· Personal crew items (small woven wall‑hangings).

Thus the diagrid is both the primary structural‑shielding element and the interior’s functional and decorative ceiling.

