Appendix D – Environmental Coupling Model: Courtyard Atrium Thermal‑Radiative Analysis

D.1 Physical Configuration

The courtyard atrium is a vertical cylindrical/polygonal shaft of diameter D_{\text{court}} = 3.0 m and height H = 8.0 m. It is gas‑filled with standard cabin atmosphere (101.3 kPa, 21% O₂, balance N₂). The atrium is surrounded on four sides by crew compartments on two decks. The roof of the atrium is the lower face of the regolith‑shielded dome, which houses the vacuum‑exposed radiator plate.

D.2 Radiative Heat Transfer Model

The atrium’s primary thermal function is to reject habitat waste heat to space via radiation. The energy balance of the atrium air is:

\rho_{\text{air}} V_{\text{court}} c_p \frac{dT_{\text{air}}}{dt} = \dot{Q}_{\text{internal}} + \dot{Q}_{\text{solar}} - \dot{Q}_{\text{rad}} - \dot{Q}_{\text{wall}}

where:

· \dot{Q}_{\text{internal}} = equipment and metabolic heat from surrounding compartments, conducted through the atrium walls (approx. 8 kW design load).
· \dot{Q}_{\text{solar}} = solar gain admitted through the fibre‑optic daylighting system, limited to 500 W by the heliostat aperture.
· \dot{Q}_{\text{rad}} = heat rejected by the roof radiator: \dot{Q}_{\text{rad}} = \epsilon \sigma A_{\text{rad}} (T_{\text{rad}}^4 - T_{\text{space}}^4), with \epsilon = 0.92, A_{\text{rad}} = 12.0 m², T_{\text{rad}} the radiator surface temperature (coupled to the fountain loop).
· \dot{Q}_{\text{wall}} = conductive loss through the 2‑m regolith shield, extremely low (< 50 W) and neglected in steady state.

The fountain coolant loop couples the atrium air to the radiator. Water enters the spiral at T_{\text{in}} = 25^\circC and exits at T_{\text{out}} \approx 22^\circC after rejecting heat to the air. The radiator is sized such that at steady state, T_{\text{air}} is maintained at 23 \pm 1^\circC.

D.3 Daylighting and Spectral Filtration

The fibre‑optic daylighting system consists of a surface heliostat with a 0.5 m² parabolic mirror, a secondary reflector, and a bundle of 200 silica optical fibres (core diameter 1 mm, NA 0.48). The collected light is transmitted through a rotary spectral filter assembly that allows:

· Full‑spectrum pass (400–700 nm) during simulated “day”,
· Blue‑enhanced (peak 480 nm) during “morning” to suppress melatonin,
· Amber‑only (peak 590 nm) during “evening” to promote relaxation,
· Off during “night”, replaced by LED array.

The light exits the fibres into a central diffusing optic shaped as a miniature muqarnas dome, which scatters the light evenly into the courtyard.

D.4 Bioregenerative Component Specifications

The vertical plant walls cover an area of 15 m² and use a hydroponic felt system irrigated by a drip‑line from the fountain loop. Plant selection:

Species Planted area Transpiration rate (g/m²/h) Cultural significance
Mint (Mentha spicata) 4 m² 50 Hospitality (tea), digestive
Coriander (Coriandrum sativum) 3 m² 45 Culinary, fertility
Rosemary (Rosmarinus officinalis) 3 m² 30 Purification, remembrance
Dwarf date palm (Phoenix dactylifera) 5 m² (2 plants) 20 Life, hospitality, shade

Total evapotranspiration load: \dot{m}_{\text{ET}} \approx 2.5 L/day, which is condensed on the dehumidifier coil and returned to the water loop. The plants contribute approximately 0.3 kg CO₂ uptake and 0.22 kg O₂ production per day.

