Appendix G – Cultural Resilience Index (CRI) and Validation Protocol

G.1 CRI Definition and Sub‑Metrics

The CRI is a composite score computed as:

\text{CRI} = w_1 \text{Coh} + w_2 \text{Emo} + w_3 \text{Perf} + w_4 \text{Rit} + w_5 \text{Sens} - w_6 \text{Stress}

Weights (initial calibration from analogue literature meta‑analysis):
w_1 = 0.20, w_2 = 0.20, w_3 = 0.15, w_4 = 0.15, w_5 = 0.15, w_6 = 0.15.

Measurement instruments:

· Coh (Cohesion): Social Network Analysis (SNA) via Bluetooth proximity and communication logs; Group Environment Questionnaire (GEQ).
· Emo (Affect): PANAS administered daily; positive affect ratio computed.
· Perf (Performance): Psychomotor Vigilance Task (PVT) reaction time and lapses; N‑back accuracy; mission task completion log.
· Rit (Ritual Coherence): Daily self‑report of participation in communal activities (prayer, meals, festivals) and satisfaction rating (1–5 Likert).
· Sens (Sensory Anchoring): Custom Environmental Quality Scale (EQS) with items on visual, acoustic, tactile, and olfactory comfort.
· Stress (Biomarker Load): Salivary cortisol (awakening, 30 min post, midday, evening); heart‑rate variability (RMSSD) from chest strap during sleep; Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index (PSQI).

All sub‑scores are normalised to a 0–100 scale before combination.

G.2 Phase I: Motif Perception & Stress Response (details)

Location: University isolation chamber (3 m × 3 m × 2.5 m) or Mars Desert Research Station upper deck.
Duration: 48 hours per condition, 1‑week washout.
Interior conditions:

· Cultural: Muqarnas‑patterned ceiling panels (foam replicas), zellige wall decals (printed at full scale), Tifinagh wayfinding strips, warm‑white lighting with amber evening shift.
· Control: Smooth grey walls, cool‑white constant lighting, no decorative elements.

Outcome measures: cortisol slope, HRV, PVT, PANAS, EQS.
Hypothesis: Cultural condition will yield a significantly flatter cortisol slope (less stress accumulation) and higher positive affect.

G.3 Phase II: Full‑Scale Integrated Simulation

Facility: Purpose‑built Moroccan Sahara Analogue Habitat, Erg Chebbi.
Modules: Two identical cylinders (7 m diameter, 8 m length), one CDG‑treated, one industrial control. CDG module includes: muqarnas diagrid (0.4 m depth), courtyard with working fountain and biowalls, mashrabiya screens, tadelakt finishes, full symbolic wayfinding.
Crews: 4 crews of 4, 30‑day missions. Randomly assigned to module, crews matched on age, gender, and prior ICE experience.
Data collection: Continuous monitoring of all CRI components. Daily surveys, weekly cognitive batteries, post‑mission debrief interviews.
Statistical plan: Linear mixed‑effects model with CRI as dependent variable, fixed effect of Module (Cultural/Control), random intercept for Crew, repeated measures over time. A priori power analysis indicates 80% power to detect medium effect (Cohen’s d = 0.5) with 4 crews per condition.
Expected outcome: Cultural module CRI > Control module CRI by at least 10 points (on 100‑point scale), with the largest contributions from Sens and Rit sub‑scores.

G.4 Phase III: Cross‑Cultural Validation

The same CDG meta‑grammar is used with cultural data packages for Navajo (Diné) and Japanese traditions, developed in consultation with respective cultural authorities. Three parallel 30‑day missions compare each cultural module against a common neutral control. The analysis tests the generalisability of the CDG and identifies culturally specific CRI weightings.

